#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078340 :question/id #uuid "8012ecda-d2f4-52de-99de-7b2ebd635661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6676" :question/problem "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}.$ After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $T_S = 25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672731 :question-generator/id #uuid "663e64eb-6b3d-5aa0-9566-a1f589c058fe" :question-generator/script "generators/0a/0a426985a3f6fd60cd1ca3792fe1abe700bd4580cef8165dd8028f3a01a3af8c/17592186078583.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72fd79b0-8552-59fe-b722-7e484092a69e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445938} {:answer/id #uuid "a014bd72-72d0-51e6-aca9-a65a27712eaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445940} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9d954e-d7a7-5d73-9350-3b59b8993edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445941} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c15d21-0615-53b3-b41f-f6213357630d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T+25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445946} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ceeca0-5ae8-54f6-8a11-ae6c66b94346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72fd79b0-8552-59fe-b722-7e484092a69e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445938} :answer-field/id #uuid "385b574a-86cb-5cbc-a02b-25339e6092c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075118}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "246dbe47-9137-5b96-af87-04ddded3bed6" :question/math-academy-id "q-105917" :question/problem "The rate at which a liquid cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$"} {:db/